---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Masthead from '../../components/Masthead.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro'
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro'
import Action from '../../components/Action.astro'
import Explain from '../../components/Explain.astro'
import GateInspector from '../../components/react/threshold/GateInspector.tsx'
import { ARKIV, ARKIV_GAS } from '../../lib/chains.ts'
import '../../styles/threshold.css'

/**
 * PATH C — THE FOUNDER
 *
 * The path with the most unfamiliar vocabulary, so it carries the most
 * hand-holding. The central claim is deliberately deflationary: founding a gated
 * archive does not require inventing a token, and most communities already own
 * everything they need.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Start an archive — The Threshold"
  description="Turn any NFT or token into the gate for your own archive — or create one. Test any contract live to see whether it can gate, then choose the only policy that matters: how much someone must hold."
>
  <Masthead slot="masthead" current="threshold" />

  <main id="main">
    <section class="act act-opening" data-tone="void" aria-labelledby="found-title">
      <div class="act-body frame">
        <SectionHead folio="04 · C" running="Start an archive" annotation="The founder" />

        <p class="path-back" data-reveal>
          <a href="/docs/threshold">← All three paths</a>
        </p>

        <h1 class="statement-colossus optical-hang" id="found-title">
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal><span>You probably already</span></span>
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal style="--delay: 90ms"><span>have the <em>key</em>.</span></span>
        </h1>

        <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--delay: 160ms">
          Founding a gated archive does not require inventing a currency. Haven asks a gate exactly
          one question — does this address hold at least this much? — so anything that can answer it
          can be a gate. If your community already has an NFT or a token, you are done choosing.
        </p>

        <a class="threshold-scroll" href="#gate-title" data-reveal style="--delay: 300ms">
          <span class="threshold-scroll-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          <span>Test your asset</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Step 1 · the gate ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="act act-pad-tall" data-tone="stock" aria-labelledby="gate-title">
      <div class="act-body frame">
        <SectionHead folio="01" running="Choose the gate" annotation="Any contract" />

        <h2 class="statement-headline optical-hang" id="gate-title">
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal><span>Test any contract</span></span>
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal style="--delay: 90ms"><span>against the gate.</span></span>
        </h2>

        <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--delay: 100ms">
          Paste any NFT or token contract and this will read it live and tell you, in plain terms,
          whether Haven can gate on it — what it is, what a balance means there, whether anything has
          been issued, and whether it answers the one call that matters.
        </p>

        <div data-reveal style="--delay: 180ms">
          <GateInspector client:visible />
        </div>

        <div data-reveal>
          <Explain term="What is a contract address?" open>
            <p>
              Every NFT collection and every token is a program deployed at an address on a
              blockchain — a string starting <code>0x</code>. That address is the collection's
              permanent identity: it cannot be renamed, transferred or faked.
            </p>
            <p>
              You can find it on any marketplace or explorer listing for the collection, usually
              labelled "contract". Haven identifies gates by address rather than by name for exactly
              that reason: names are editorial, addresses are not.
            </p>
          </Explain>

          <Explain term="What is the difference between an NFT and a token here?">
            <p>
              An NFT collection (ERC-721) holds distinct items, so a balance is a count: holding two
              means holding two pieces. A threshold of one means "any piece from the collection".
            </p>
            <p>
              A token (ERC-20) is divisible, so a balance is an amount. Thresholds become quantities —
              "hold at least 5,000" — and Haven converts for the token's decimal places itself, so you
              never have to write out base units.
            </p>
          </Explain>

          <Explain term="Can I gate on something my community does not control?">
            <p>
              Technically yes: gating only reads a balance, and reading is permissionless. Practically
              it is a bad idea. If you do not influence the asset, you have no say over who can reach
              your archive, and a large holder can hand access around without you.
            </p>
            <p>
              Gate on something your community actually holds and cares about. The asset becomes the
              membership boundary, so it should be one the community recognises as its own.
            </p>
          </Explain>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Step 2 · if you have nothing yet ────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="act act-pad-tall" data-tone="void" aria-labelledby="create-title">
      <div class="act-body frame">
        <SectionHead folio="02" running="If you have none yet" annotation="Optional" />

        <h2 class="statement-headline optical-hang" id="create-title">
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal><span>Making one is a</span></span>
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal style="--delay: 90ms"><span>smaller job than it sounds.</span></span>
        </h2>

        <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--delay: 100ms">
          Haven does not issue assets and has no opinion about how you make yours. These are the
          standard routes, and none of them involve us — deploy with whatever you already trust, then
          come back and test the address above.
        </p>

        <ol class="path-steps" data-reveal style="--delay: 160ms">
          <li class="path-step">
            <span class="folio">01</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="path-step-title">Decide which kind you need</h3>
              <p class="path-step-body">
                A collection if membership should be a thing people hold one of — a pass, an edition, a
                piece of the work. A token if membership should be a quantity, so that holding more
                means more, and small holders can be excluded by a threshold.
              </p>
              <p class="path-step-detail">
                Most archives want a collection. Quantities invite the question of how much is enough,
                and that argument tends to outlive the archive.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="path-step">
            <span class="folio">02</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="path-step-title">Generate and deploy it</h3>
              <p class="path-step-body">
                Standard contracts are a solved problem: you do not need to write Solidity, and you
                certainly should not write it for this. Generate an audited standard implementation,
                or use a platform that deploys one for you.
              </p>
              <div class="path-links">
                <a href="https://wizard.openzeppelin.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">
                  OpenZeppelin Wizard<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
                </a>
                <a href="https://thirdweb.com/explore" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">
                  thirdweb contracts<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
                </a>
                <a href="https://manifold.xyz/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">
                  Manifold<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
                </a>
              </div>
              <p class="path-step-detail">
                These are widely used third parties, not endorsements or integrations. Haven never
                sees the deployment; it only ever reads the finished contract.
              </p>
              <Explain term="What does deploying cost?">
                <p>
                  A one-off gas fee on whichever chain you choose, paid to the network rather than to
                  any company. It varies with congestion and with the chain — the same contract is
                  dramatically cheaper on Base or Optimism than on Ethereum mainnet.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Haven reads gates on all three, so the chain is your decision and cost is a fair
                  basis for making it.
                </p>
              </Explain>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="path-step">
            <span class="folio">03</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="path-step-title">Issue some before you publish</h3>
              <p class="path-step-body">
                A gate over a supply of zero excludes everybody, including you. Mint to yourself and to
                the community first, then test the address above — the inspector will warn you if
                nothing has been issued.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Step 3 · publish ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="act act-pad-tall" data-tone="stock" aria-labelledby="then-title">
      <div class="act-body frame">
        <SectionHead folio="03" running="Then publish into it" annotation="One blocked step" />

        <h2 class="statement-headline optical-hang" id="then-title">
          <span class="mask-line" data-reveal><span>The gate is the easy part.</span></span>
        </h2>

        <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--delay: 100ms">
          With a gate chosen and a threshold set, founding is finished — what remains is publishing,
          which is the same work for you as for anyone else contributing to a community's archive.
        </p>

        <ol class="path-steps" data-reveal style="--delay: 160ms">
          <li class="path-step">
            <span class="folio">01</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="path-step-title">Follow the publisher's path</h3>
              <p class="path-step-body">
                FIL for Filecoin gas, USDFC for the storage, {ARKIV_GAS.symbol} for gas on the index,
                and <code>haven-cli</code> to encrypt locally and pin the ciphertext. Your gate address
                and threshold are parameters to that process, not a separate registration.
              </p>
              <div class="path-links">
                <a href="/docs/threshold/publish">The publisher's path →</a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>

          <li class="path-step path-step-blocked">
            <span class="folio">02</span>
            <div>
              <h3 class="path-step-title">
                Announce it
                <span class="label path-blocked-mark">{ARKIV.statusShort}</span>
              </h3>
              <p class="path-step-body">
                Registering your archive as an entity in Arkiv — a transaction paid in{' '}
                {ARKIV_GAS.symbol} — is what makes it findable — it binds the
                archive to your gate, records its piece CIDs, and lets the Atlas attribute the bytes to
                your community. The protocol requires this layer; it is how any client knows your
                archive exists.
              </p>
              <p class="path-step-detail">
                Arkiv retired its {ARKIV.codename} testnet on {ARKIV.retiredOn} and the next public
                one is expected {ARKIV.nextPublicTestnet}, so there is currently nowhere to write the
                entity. Your archive would still exist, still be encrypted, still be proven, and still
                open for anyone holding the gate asset who knows where to look — what waits on the
                index is discovery.
              </p>
              <dl class="path-facts">
                <div>
                  <dt class="label">Retired endpoint</dt>
                  <dd class="addr">{ARKIV.rpc}</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <dt class="label">Next public testnet</dt>
                  <dd>{ARKIV.nextPublicTestnet}</dd>
                </div>
              </dl>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="threshold-actions" data-reveal style="--delay: 240ms">
          <Action href="/docs/threshold/publish" variant="sealed" size="large">Go to the publisher's path</Action>
          <Action href="/docs/atlas" variant="keyline" glyph="none">See existing communities</Action>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
